# When the attribution gate started asking, and every time that moved.
#
# The gate is a forward ratchet: it refuses a page the diff names and never a
# page already written, because the record it inherited has 206 pages that would
# fail and relitigating them is the sweep this lane's own finding says does not
# work. That is honest exactly as long as the ref cannot be moved quietly - a
# pin nudged forward past an inconvenient page clears it while every run still
# prints "pass".
#
# So this file is the ref, it is committed, and it is append-only. The live pin
# is the LAST line. Three rules stand behind it, all of them in
# `sighting.py`:
#
#   1. every run prints the live pin and its age, pass or fail, so the ref is
#      never off-screen;
#   2. a working-tree copy of this file that differs from the committed one
#      refuses the run - moving the pin costs a reviewed commit, never a local
#      convenience;
#   3. `--since` on the command line may only reach FURTHER BACK than the pin.
#      An override that asks about fewer pages is refused, so no flag loosens
#      the gate.
#
# Move it with `sighting.py --pin <ref> --because "<why>"`, which appends a line
# and prints, before it writes, exactly which currently-refused pages the move
# stops asking about. A move that clears pages has to be typed next to the
# number of pages it clears.
#
# date        commit     clears  why
2026-08-06    f7ba400    0       the commit the gate was turned on at
